    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the solution <m>u(x,y)</m> to Laplace's equation
                <m>{\nabla^2 u = 0}</m> in a <m>4\times 8</m> rectangle where
                <m>{0\le x\le 4}</m> and <m>{0\le y \le 8}</m> where <m>u=0</m>
                along the top, bottom, and left, and <m>{u(4,y) = y}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the solution <m>u(r,\theta)</m> to Laplace's equation
                <m>\nabla^2 u=0</m> in a disk of radius 3 where the boundary
                condition is
                <md>
                    f(\theta) = \begin{cases} 2\pi-\theta &amp; \text{if } 0\leq \theta \leq \pi \\ \theta &amp; \text{if } -\pi \leq \theta \leq 0 \end{cases}
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the solution <m>u(x,y,t)</m> to the wave equation
                <md>
                    \pdtwo{u}{t} = \nabla^2u
                </md>
                on a <m>5\times5</m> square drum where <m>u = 0</m> on the
                boundary (the membrane is clamped), the membrane is initially at
                rest (<m>{\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(x,y,0) = 0}</m>), and
                the initial position of the membrane is given by:
                <md>
                    u(x,y,0) = \sin\left({\pi x}\right) \sin\left(\frac{\pi y}{5}\right).
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve Laplace's equation <m>{\nabla^2u=0}</m> for the function
                <m>{u(x,y,z)}</m> in a 3-dimensional cube whose sides have length
                2.
                Assume that <m>u=0</m> on five of the sides, and
                <m>{u(x,y,2) = 1}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider a rectangular flag that has length <m>19</m> and 
                height <m>10</m>.  (Fun fact, there are strict requirements for 
                US flags, and this ratio is one of them.)
                Assume the left side of the flag is fixed at 0, while the other three 
                sides are free.  That is we have the wave equation with the following 
                boundary conditions:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\pdtwo{u}{t} \amp = \nabla^2 u </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(0,y,t)\amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{x}(19, y,t) \amp = 0</mrow>
                    <mrow> \pd{u}{y}(x,0,t)\amp =0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{y}(x,10,t) \amp = 0</mrow>
                </md>
                Find the general solution.  A huge help to you - on Homework 4 you 
                did a problem in one-dimension that results in a Sturm-Liouville problem
                that may be of interest to you.  There should be two Sturm-Liouville
                problems that pop up in this solution, and they are not the same...
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <em>Optional read, and only if it interests you!</em>  Fun fact: problem 5 is a simplified version of Boundary Conditions of the Third Kind, 
        where <m>{X(0) = 0}</m> but <m>{X'(L) = -h X(L)}</m>.  We just took <m>{h = 0}</m>.  For the heat equation, 
        <m>{h\gt 0}</m> corresponds to Newton's Law of Cooling and so has physical 
        relevance, and similar for the wave equation <m>{h\gt 0}</m> has the physical 
        meaning of a restoring force, the so-called elastic boundary condition.  So in 
        physical problems we would focus on <m>{h\ge 0}</m>.  Though mathematically there 
        is some interesting behavior with <m>{h \lt 0}</m>.  For instance, 
        this would correspond to a vibrating string with a destabilizing force 
        at the right end, or for the heat equation you can think of it 
        as thermal energy being constantly put into the rod through the right end.
        To solve this Sturm-Liouville problem you would still 
        need to separately check if <m>k \lt 0, k = 0, k \gt 0</m>, but you 
        have an extra complexity where you also need to account for <m>h</m> 
        being being non-negative (a physical situation) or negative (a non-physical situation).  There are more than 3 cases!
    </p>
</worksheet>